🧩 Integration Architecture and File Responsibilities
Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy

This is the main pipeline entry point for the Correction execution flow.

It is the file Jenkins calls directly when the job starts.

Main responsibilities
Define the Katalon project file to execute
Define which Correction suites belong to this build
Configure startup behavior
Configure browser and suite timeout
Load all helper files
Orchestrate the full flow from startup to shutdown
Optionally trigger the next Jenkins job later, if needed
What should be changed here

This is the main file to update when you want to:

Add or remove suites from the Correction build
Change the execution order of suites
Change the Katalon .prj file name
Change the browser type
Change suite timeout
Change startup task name
Enable or disable next job trigger
Important note

This file should stay focused on configuration and orchestration, not detailed technical logic.

JenkinsAppManager.groovy

This file is responsible for the application lifecycle used by Jenkins.

Main responsibilities
Start the application through the scheduled task
Wait the configured amount of time before execution continues
Optionally validate startup readiness by ports or health URL
Stop the application after execution finishes
Kill only the intended application processes
Avoid stopping unrelated Jenkins or system processes
Why this file exists

Without this separation, application startup and shutdown logic would make the main pipeline file too large and hard to maintain.

When to change this file

Update this file when you need to:

Change how the application is started
Change how startup validation works
Change how ports are checked
Improve shutdown safety
Fix issues where application processes remain open after build completion
Important note

This file must contain only start and stop logic.
It must not contain pipeline configuration such as suite lists or job settings.

JenkinsConsoleView.groovy

This file is responsible for the human readable console output shown in Jenkins.

Main responsibilities
Print suite start blocks
Print suite result summaries
Print running summary after each suite
Print final pipeline summary
Format durations
Format short and clean output for easier reading
Why this file exists

Jenkins logs can become very noisy and difficult to read.
This helper centralizes the visual output so the console remains cleaner and more structured.

When to change this file

Update this file when you want to:

Improve console readability
Add or remove summary fields
Change icons or visual formatting
Show more or fewer case and step details
JenkinsLiveLogger.groovy

This is the file responsible for running each suite and collecting live execution information.

Main responsibilities
Execute one Katalon suite
Launch Katalon with the correct parameters
Stream useful live output into Jenkins console
Read execution0.log during execution
Extract meaningful scenario and step messages
Detect timeout
Find the best JUnit file
Find the best screenshot
Create metadata for the suite
Generate synthetic JUnit if the suite fails without generating one
Why this file exists

This is the technical core of suite execution.
It isolates the heavy logic related to Katalon runtime, logs, metadata, and evidence discovery.

What kind of data it produces

For each suite it produces or resolves:

Suite status
Exit code
Duration
Executed / Passed / Failed / Skipped counts
Failure reason
Screenshot path
Log path
Case summary
Step summary
When to change this file

Update this file when you need to:

Improve live Jenkins console visibility
Change how execution0.log is parsed
Change how screenshots are selected
Improve failure reason extraction
Change timeout behavior
Adjust metadata creation
JenkinsPaths.groovy

This file centralizes path calculation used across the Jenkins integration.

Main responsibilities
Resolve current workspace
Build the project file path
Build the Reports root path
Build the logs folder path
Build the suite artifacts path
Why this file exists

It avoids repeating hardcoded path logic across multiple files.

When to change this file

Update this file when:

Folder structure changes
Reports location changes
Artifact location changes
Project path resolution needs to be adjusted
JenkinsResultStore.groovy

This file is responsible for persisting and reading suite execution metadata.

Main responsibilities
Read suite .meta.properties files
Parse stored metadata
Split case and step summaries
Save the full suite result list as JSON
Reload suite results later in the pipeline
Why this file exists

The pipeline needs a stable way to carry suite result data across stages and into post actions.

When to change this file

Update this file when you need to:

Add new stored metadata fields
Change metadata parsing rules
Change the JSON structure used to persist results
🔄 End to End Execution Flow

This section explains how the Jenkins pipeline behaves during execution, from bootstrap to final shutdown.

1. Bootstrap support files

The pipeline begins by doing a lightweight checkout containing the Jenkins support files.

Purpose
Ensure helper files are available before the full repository checkout
Allow validation of the integration files early in the build
2. Validate support files

The pipeline validates the presence of the required Jenkins helper files and the Katalon executable.

Purpose
Fail fast if the environment is broken
Avoid reaching suite execution with missing support files
3. Start application

The pipeline calls JenkinsAppManager.groovy to start the application through the configured scheduled task.

Purpose
Bring backend and frontend up before the test execution starts
Important behavior

Depending on configuration, startup can be:

Fixed wait only
Port validation
Health URL validation
Combined validation
4. Full checkout repository

After startup, the pipeline performs the full checkout of the repository.

Purpose
Retrieve the full automation project
Retrieve test suites, objects, keywords, settings, and reports structure
5. Validate project file

The pipeline checks if the configured Katalon project file exists.

Example
gaia-portal.prj
Purpose
Fail early if the wrong .prj file name is configured
6. Normalize runtime settings

The pipeline updates runtime related files before suite execution.

Purpose
Disable unstable rerun behaviors
Disable self healing related flags when needed
Keep execution more deterministic in Jenkins
7. Prepare artifacts

The pipeline prepares the Reports structure and initializes result persistence.

Purpose
Ensure reports and logs folders are clean
Ensure suite results can be saved progressively
8. Execute suites

The pipeline loops through all configured Correction suites.

For each suite:

Print suite header
Run the suite through JenkinsLiveLogger.groovy
Read suite metadata
Resolve screenshot and log
Save suite results
Print suite result
Print running summary
Important behavior

If one suite fails:

Its stage is marked as failure
The pipeline still keeps the result information
Final summary can still be generated
9. Final summary

In post execution, the pipeline loads all saved suite results and prints the final summary.

Purpose
Provide one final consolidated view of the execution outcome
10. Stop application

The pipeline then calls JenkinsAppManager.groovy again to stop the application.

Purpose
Free the environment for the next build
Avoid keeping backend or frontend processes open
Important note

This is one of the most sensitive parts of the pipeline.
If stop logic is too broad, it can accidentally kill Jenkins related processes instead of only the application.

11. Publish artifacts and JUnit

The pipeline archives screenshots and publishes JUnit results.

Purpose
Make failures visible in Jenkins
Keep evidence accessible after execution
Preserve JUnit compatibility for reporting
12. Optional next job trigger

If enabled in the future, the pipeline can trigger another Jenkins job as a separate build.

Current status

For now, this remains disabled with:

triggerNextJob : false
nextJobName : ''
🗂️ Current Correction Build Scope

The current Correction build is designed to execute the following suites in one single Jenkins build:

Correction- Add Correction
Correction- Computation Selection
Correction- Corrected Duplication
Correction- Correction Details
Correction- Download Invalid Records

These suites are configured inside Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy.

⚙️ Main Configuration Area

The most important editable section is the PIPELINE_CONFIG block inside Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy.

This block currently centralizes:

Project file name
List of suites
Next job trigger configuration
Application startup task
Startup wait
Startup validation settings
Stop ports
Process patterns
Browser
Suite timeout

This means most day to day maintenance can be done from one single place.

🛠️ Troubleshooting Guide
Pipeline stops before any suite starts

Possible causes:

Missing Jenkins helper file
Katalon executable path invalid
Project .prj file name incorrect
Startup task failed
Port or health validation too strict
First places to check
Validate Support Files
Validate Project File
Start Application
Application starts but suites do not run

Possible causes:

Katalon project file not found
Broken test suite path
Runtime normalization issue
Build failed before reaching execution stage
First places to check
Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy
JenkinsPaths.groovy
JenkinsLiveLogger.groovy
Suite runs but console is hard to understand

Possible causes:

Noisy raw Katalon output
Missing step extraction
Insufficient summary formatting
First places to check
JenkinsConsoleView.groovy
JenkinsLiveLogger.groovy
Pipeline finishes but app processes remain open

Possible causes:

Stop logic too weak
Process patterns not matching the application
Ports not being targeted correctly
First place to check
JenkinsAppManager.groovy
Pipeline loses Jenkins node connection while stopping application

Possible causes:

Stop logic too broad
Killing generic cmd.exe, java.exe, or node.exe
Accidentally killing Jenkins remoting related process
First place to check
JenkinsAppManager.groovy
Screenshot exists but is blank or white

Possible causes:

Screenshot captured after browser became blank
Screenshot captured too late
Failure listener capturing after state loss
First place to check

This is usually not Jenkins first.
It is more likely in the Katalon evidence capture timing.

🧠 Recommended Maintenance Rules
Keep Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy focused on orchestration

Do not place heavy technical execution logic there.

Keep JenkinsAppManager.groovy focused only on startup and shutdown

Do not place suite configuration or pipeline settings there.

Keep process matching specific

Avoid generic patterns that can affect Jenkins infrastructure.

Keep path logic centralized

Do not duplicate path calculations in multiple files.

Keep result persistence stable

Metadata and result storage should remain predictable so post actions can safely load them.

📌 Suggested Mental Model

You can think of the Jenkins integration like this:

Jenkinsfile_Correction.groovy
= the orchestrator
JenkinsAppManager.groovy
= the environment controller
JenkinsLiveLogger.groovy
= the suite executor and live collector
JenkinsConsoleView.groovy
= the console presenter
JenkinsPaths.groovy
= the path resolver
JenkinsResultStore.groovy
= the execution memory
✅ Final objective of this structure

This Jenkins structure is designed to make the automation execution:

easier to configure
easier to read
easier to troubleshoot
safer to evolve
more reusable for future pipeline files



